  "step_1": "The decision reason evaluates lotteries based on the average of 'probable' outcomes. A outcome is considered probable if its probability is greater than 1 divided by the number of outcomes. Lottery A has one outcome (500 Euros with 100% probability), so 1 divided by 1 is 1. Thus, the only outcome (500 Euros) has probability 100%, which is greater than 1, so it is probable. Lottery B has two outcomes: 1000 Euros with 50% probability and 0 Euros with 50% probability. The threshold is 1 divided by 2 = 0.5. So, outcomes with probability > 0.5 are considered probable. Thus, 1000 Euros (50%) is not greater than 0.5, so it is not probable. 0 Euros (50%) is also not greater than 0.5, so it is not probable. Therefore, neither outcome in B is probable. Thus, the average of probable outcomes in A is 500 Euros (only one probable outcome), and in B it is 0. So, the reason prefers A because it has a higher average of probable outcomes (500 > 0). The reason is applicable to this decision problem.",
